1
00:00:00,256 --> 00:00:04,596
Five coins, two minutes, and a result when the clock reaches zero.

2
00:00:04,779 --> 00:00:09,918
Our woodland now ends in Victory with five coins, or Failed when
the goal is missed.

3
00:00:09,962 --> 00:00:15,674
We'll build the start, countdown, pickup rule and settlement with
native controls and node graphs.

4
00:00:23,199 --> 00:00:28,736
We'll define a Global Timer, show it in the interface, and connect
a Start button to a Player graph.

5
00:00:28,879 --> 00:00:33,176
Then we'll allow pickups only during the run and choose the result
at zero.

6
00:00:33,487 --> 00:00:38,727
Keep the working progress-bar lesson open as a separate copy, so
we can build on its tested counter.

7
00:00:39,538 --> 00:00:45,073
The Global Timer has a definition, an entity that runs it, and an
optional interface display.

8
00:00:45,213 --> 00:00:47,932
Timer Management defines its name and duration.

9
00:00:48,072 --> 00:00:52,696
Our Player carries the running timer. The Timer control shows that
Player's time.

10
00:00:52,780 --> 00:00:58,008
Keeping those three roles connected prevents a countdown on one
entity and a display reading another.

11
00:00:58,718 --> 00:01:04,798
Open the System Menu and choose Timer Management. Add a new timer,
then rename it Round Timer.

12
00:01:04,887 --> 00:01:08,864
Choose Countdown and set Duration to one hundred and twenty
seconds.

13
00:01:09,111 --> 00:01:11,574
This name is the reference we'll use in the graph.

14
00:01:11,756 --> 00:01:15,639
Check the spelling, and keep this timer distinct from any others
in your save.

15
00:01:28,737 --> 00:01:32,117
In Manage UI Control Groups, open Default Layout.

16
00:01:32,151 --> 00:01:38,560
Choose Add UI control, then Prefabricated Template, then Timer.
Open its Functions settings.

17
00:01:38,679 --> 00:01:44,168
Change Type to Countdown and choose Round Timer. This filters the
list to the matching timer type.

18
00:01:44,201 --> 00:01:47,652
Leave room around the existing coin bar and original game
controls.

19
00:01:58,732 --> 00:02:04,746
Change Source Entity from Stage to Player Themself. Our controller
will start the timer on that Player.

20
00:02:04,811 --> 00:02:10,538
The official Global Timer guide explains that an inactive timer
displays zero minutes and zero seconds.

21
00:02:10,742 --> 00:02:14,271
That initial display doesn't mean a fresh two-minute run has
already ended.

22
00:02:14,326 --> 00:02:16,632
It can mean the timer hasn't been activated yet.

23
00:02:17,284 --> 00:02:21,795
Open Default Player through Edit Player and inspect its Global
Timer component.

24
00:02:21,892 --> 00:02:27,756
In this save, it is present, with an empty reference list. Leave
Round Timer out of that list.

25
00:02:27,885 --> 00:02:32,076
A component reference activates a Global Timer when the entity is
created.

26
00:02:32,322 --> 00:02:36,118
We want the graph to activate it after the player requests Start.

27
00:02:40,389 --> 00:02:46,692
Back in Default Layout, add a Custom Button and label it Start.
Set Key Type to Interactive Event.

28
00:02:46,789 --> 00:02:52,729
For this PC lesson, map it to F, shown as Pick Up or Interact in
the keyboard-and-mouse selector.

29
00:02:52,890 --> 00:02:58,273
This is the stage control's mapping. Consider other interactions
if you use that key in your own game.

30
00:03:08,361 --> 00:03:13,975
Select the Start instance and note its Index. We'll compare the
interaction event against that value.

31
00:03:14,108 --> 00:03:20,091
Use the Index on your own layout instance, rather than copying my
generated number or a template's number.

32
00:03:20,252 --> 00:03:24,506
If the button is replaced later, read its new Index and update the
comparison.

33
00:03:25,301 --> 00:03:32,307
Add two predefined standard Boolean variables to Default Player:
RoundStarted and RoundActive, both No.

34
00:03:32,469 --> 00:03:36,889
RoundStarted remembers that this challenge instance has accepted a
start request.

35
00:03:37,066 --> 00:03:41,385
RoundActive says whether pickups are currently allowed. They have
different jobs.

36
00:03:41,546 --> 00:03:46,894
At the end we'll turn Active off while Started stays Yes, so
another press can't restart this run.

37
00:03:49,202 --> 00:03:54,118
Create a Server Entity Node Graph called EP06 Round Controller.

38
00:03:54,151 --> 00:03:58,811
Mount it on Default Player through Edit Player and its Node Graph
tab, then save.

39
00:03:58,972 --> 00:04:02,594
The UI interaction event is received by the Player's node graph.

40
00:04:02,740 --> 00:04:07,092
This controller belongs on the Player, rather than on a scenery
object or a coin.

41
00:04:12,238 --> 00:04:16,642
Add When UI Control Group Is Triggered. For a small first test,

42
00:04:16,718 --> 00:04:22,498
convert its UI Control Group Index to String and print it. Select
the controller in the native Log,

43
00:04:22,532 --> 00:04:27,576
then press Start during a private test. Our printed value matches
the Start instance.

44
00:04:27,737 --> 00:04:31,226
This checks the event and mounting before adding the full start
chain.

45
00:04:37,061 --> 00:04:40,577
In the working controller, compare the event's UI Control Group

46
00:04:40,602 --> 00:04:44,473
Index with the Start instance Index using an Integer comparison.

47
00:04:44,655 --> 00:04:50,359
Feed that result into Double Branch and continue from Yes. Other
interface interactions should stop here.

48
00:04:50,392 --> 00:04:55,877
Use Get Self Entity for this graph's Player, and keep that target
consistent through the start actions.

49
00:04:58,094 --> 00:05:02,126
Read Self's RoundStarted as Boolean and add another Double Branch.

50
00:05:02,256 --> 00:05:07,833
Continue only from No. Set RoundStarted to Yes, then set
RoundActive to Yes.

51
00:05:07,973 --> 00:05:14,112
These are execution steps, connected in that order. The first flag
closes further start requests;

52
00:05:14,145 --> 00:05:17,821
the second opens the existing pickup rule for the running
challenge.

53
00:05:18,666 --> 00:05:24,200
Continue to Start Global Timer, target Self, and enter Round Timer
as the Timer Name.

54
00:05:24,340 --> 00:05:28,659
Finish this small chain with Print String, using round started as
its message.

55
00:05:28,820 --> 00:05:32,961
Save the graph and scene. We're starting the predefined Global
Timer here,

56
00:05:32,994 --> 00:05:36,559
so choose that node rather than the separate interval-based Start
Timer system.

57
00:05:37,252 --> 00:05:41,219
Open the pickup graph. Resolve the entering character to its
Player as before.

58
00:05:41,412 --> 00:05:46,712
Read that Player's RoundActive Boolean and place a Double Branch
before the existing PickedUp check.

59
00:05:46,831 --> 00:05:52,096
Connect the event's execution to this new branch. Yes continues to
the original pickup chain;

60
00:05:52,121 --> 00:05:57,413
No has no award or destruction path. Remove the old execution
bypass.

61
00:06:00,295 --> 00:06:05,091
Return to the Player controller and add When Global Timer Is
Triggered. Compare its Timer

62
00:06:05,116 --> 00:06:10,809
Name with Round Timer, then continue only from Yes. This event is
the countdown reaching zero.

63
00:06:10,979 --> 00:06:16,037
The name check keeps our result chain specific if another Global
Timer is added to the project later.

64
00:06:16,828 --> 00:06:23,310
Read Self's RoundActive and branch again. Continue only if it is
Yes, then set RoundActive to No.

65
00:06:23,416 --> 00:06:28,724
Close the run before the settlement actions. After this step, the
coin graph's new gate no

66
00:06:28,749 --> 00:06:34,210
longer allows awards. The controller can now read the final count
and choose one result path.

67
00:06:34,904 --> 00:06:40,139
Read Self's Coins and CoinGoal as Integers. Compare Coins greater
than or equal

68
00:06:40,164 --> 00:06:44,909
to CoinGoal and feed the Boolean result into Double Branch. Our
goal is five.

69
00:06:45,028 --> 00:06:49,999
Reusing the goal from the progress bar keeps its full value and
the challenge rule aligned.

70
00:06:50,130 --> 00:06:54,135
We don't need a particular tree position or a pavilion trigger for
this objective.

71
00:06:54,942 --> 00:06:59,879
On Yes, use Set Player Settlement Success Status for Self and
choose Victory.

72
00:06:59,977 --> 00:07:03,170
On No, use the same node with the failure option.

73
00:07:03,358 --> 00:07:08,360
The client in this recording labels it Failed. Keep these
execution paths separate.

74
00:07:08,521 --> 00:07:13,075
Only the selected branch should run, and each branch will prepare
its own settlement data.

75
00:07:19,865 --> 00:07:24,823
After the status node on each path, add Set Player Settlement
Scoreboard Data Display.

76
00:07:24,856 --> 00:07:30,378
Target Self, use data order one, enter Coins as the name, and
supply a fresh read of

77
00:07:30,403 --> 00:07:35,288
the Player's current Coins value. That is the score we want the
result card to display.

78
00:07:35,364 --> 00:07:38,436
The HUD binding alone doesn't create this settlement entry.

79
00:07:39,138 --> 00:07:45,207
Continue each selected path to Settle Stage. Setting a success
status and score prepares the information;

80
00:07:45,262 --> 00:07:48,407
this final action starts the stage settlement process.

81
00:07:48,675 --> 00:07:53,627
Save both the graph and editing scene. Read the end chain once:
correct timer,

82
00:07:53,652 --> 00:07:59,713
active run, close pickups, compare goal, set status and score,
then settle.

83
00:08:00,489 --> 00:08:03,592
Start a fresh private test and leave Start untouched.

84
00:08:03,732 --> 00:08:08,485
The coin bar is empty and the timer is inactive at zero. Walk
through the first coin.

85
00:08:08,510 --> 00:08:13,045
In our test it remains, with the counter still at zero. This
checks that the new gate

86
00:08:13,070 --> 00:08:17,573
is connected before the old award chain, rather than sitting
beside a working bypass.

87
00:08:18,461 --> 00:08:23,258
Return to the starting area and press F. The timer begins its
two-minute countdown,

88
00:08:23,283 --> 00:08:28,985
and coins can now be collected. In a separate start check, I press
F again while the clock is running.

89
00:08:29,061 --> 00:08:32,526
It continues counting down instead of jumping back to two minutes.

90
00:08:32,816 --> 00:08:35,931
RoundStarted is what protects that accepted start path.

91
00:08:39,437 --> 00:08:43,606
In the winning run, collect all five coins and watch the bar fill.

92
00:08:43,832 --> 00:08:49,857
Then let the full countdown reach zero naturally. The original
recording keeps the entire wait;

93
00:08:49,976 --> 00:08:52,810
this lesson cuts to later moments in the same run.

94
00:08:52,937 --> 00:08:57,128
The result is Victory, and the settlement card shows Coins, five.

95
00:09:03,489 --> 00:09:09,480
Choose Retry Challenge. Check the coins, the empty bar and the
inactive timer before starting again.

96
00:09:09,505 --> 00:09:16,786
Our new instance has zero Coins and no active countdown. Pressing
F begins a new run. Checking this starting

97
00:09:16,811 --> 00:09:22,973
state matters, because a correct first run can still hide an
incorrect retry or initialization path.

98
00:09:23,755 --> 00:09:28,756
This time, leave the coins alone and wait through another complete
two-minute countdown.

99
00:09:28,832 --> 00:09:32,724
At zero, the result is Failed, with Coins, zero.

100
00:09:32,928 --> 00:09:37,230
The waiting is shortened in the edit again. A winning test checks
one branch;

101
00:09:37,349 --> 00:09:42,177
this second natural run checks the other result using the same
timer and settlement rule.

102
00:09:45,686 --> 00:09:50,055
If the clock stays at zero, check the timer definition, the Player
component,

103
00:09:50,080 --> 00:09:53,460
the UI's Player source, and the Start event in that order.

104
00:09:53,621 --> 00:09:59,600
If it starts too soon, inspect the component's timer references.
If pickups happen before Start,

105
00:09:59,697 --> 00:10:03,913
follow the event's white execution line and look for a route
around RoundActive.

106
00:10:04,738 --> 00:10:11,062
Before moving on, test an untouched coin before Start, a repeated
start request, a natural success,

107
00:10:11,138 --> 00:10:16,482
a retry, and a natural failure. Point to the two different flags
and explain their jobs.

108
00:10:16,514 --> 00:10:22,378
Keep your working copy. We now have a complete timed loop that can
drive meaningful achievements next time.
